answer:
The choice \(\delta=\min\!\left\{\frac{c}{2},\,\frac{c^{2}}{2\varepsilon}\right\}\) is designed to control two aspects of the expression \(\bigl|\frac{1}{x}-\frac{1}{c}\bigr|\). First, requiring \(|x-c|<\frac{c}{2}\) ensures that \(x\) stays away from zero: since \(c\in(0,1)\), we have \(x>c-\frac{c}{2}=\frac{c}{2}>0\). This gives a lower bound on the denominator \(x\) and allows us to estimate\n    \[
    \bigl|\tfrac{1}{x}-\tfrac{1}{c}\bigr|
      =\frac{|x-c|}{|x\,c|}
      \le \frac{|x-c|}{\frac{c}{2}\,c}
      =\frac{2|x-c|}{c^{2}}.
    \]\n    Second, the condition \(|x-c|<\frac{c^{2}}{2\varepsilon}\) ensures that the right‑hand side of the above inequality is less than \(\varepsilon\): substituting the bound on \(|x-c|\) gives\n    \[
    \frac{2|x-c|}{c^{2}}
      < \frac{2}{c^{2}}\cdot\frac{c^{2}}{2\varepsilon}
      =\varepsilon.
    \]\n    By taking \(\delta\) to be the minimum of these two numbers, we simultaneously guarantee that \(x\) stays in the interval \((0,1)\) (so the function is defined) and that the difference \(\bigl|\frac{1}{x}-\frac{1}{c}\bigr|\) is smaller than the prescribed \(\varepsilon\). Thus the chosen \(\delta\) satisfies the \(\varepsilon\)–\(\delta\) condition for continuity at the point \(c\).
